{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he would not listen to her, to lie with her.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): even without intercourse. <b>To be with her</b> — in intercourse, for we find the term ביאה in the language of הויה, as in what is written (Ruth 1:12): ״גם הייתי הלילה לאיש״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: she said to him, 'Why do you not listen to me? No one knows us.' He said to her, 'The unmarried women among you are forbidden to us — a married woman all the more so!' A parable — what is the matter like? Like a gentile who said to an Israelite, 'I have fine and choice food to feed you.' He said to him, 'What is it?' He said to him, 'Pork.' He said to him, 'Emptiest one in the world! Your slaughtered meat from a clean animal is forbidden to us — pork all the more so!' So too said Joseph: 'Your unmarried women are forbidden to me — a married woman all the more so!'" "<b>And he would not listen to her</b> — to lie with her in this world; <b>to be with her</b> — in Gehenna. Three men are trustworthy: (1) the householder who separates his tithes properly — he is not suspected regarding terumah and ma'asrot, and you have no greater trust than this. (2) A bachelor who dwells in a neighborhood of harlots and does not sin — you have no greater trust than this. (3) A poor man into whose keeping a deposit was placed and he is not suspected of it — you have no greater trust than this. You find no one as trustworthy as Joseph, who was in Egypt, concerning whom it is written (Ezekiel 23:20): ״אשר בשר חמורים בשרם״, and he was seventeen years old and she enticed him every day, and he was not suspected of illicit relations. Three conquered their inclination, and they are these: Joseph, Boaz, Palti son of Laish. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Sanhedrin: the might of Joseph is the humility of Boaz — for with Joseph it was by day, and with Boaz by night, as it is written (Ruth 3:8): ״ויהי בחצי הלילה ויחרד האיש וילפת והנה אשה שוכבת מרגלותיו״. And he conquered his inclination and did not approach her at all until after marriage. The might of Boaz is the humility of Palti son of Laish — for Boaz was one night, but Palti son of Laish was a long time. For at the hour that an evil spirit came upon Saul to give him Michal his daughter, as it is said (1 Samuel 25:44): ״ושאול נתן את מיכל בתו אשת דוד לפלטי בן ליש אשר מגלים״, and Palti son of Laish knew that kiddushin do not take hold for a married woman and that Saul coerced his daughter and gave her to him, and because he feared Saul he showed himself as though he had taken her, for he had no desire for her. And once it became clear to David that Saul had coerced her and that Palti son of Laish had no desire for her, after Saul died he sent to Saul's son and said (2 Samuel 3:14): ״תנה את אשתי את מיכל אשר ארשתי לי במאה ערלות פלשתים״. Therefore Scripture specifies and says (there): ״ויקחה מעם איש״ — and it does not say ״מעם אישה״. And what is written (there): ״וילך אתה אישה״ — its meaning is that Palti went to escort her to go to David her husband. And what is written (there): ״הלוך ובכה אחריה עד בחורים״ — because he feared David, lest he not believe him and repay him with evil."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:19:02.449239742Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}